<h2>How much sun does each type of solar camera need?</h2>
<p>The answer depends on how much energy the camera uses per day and how big the panel is. As a rough guide, assuming a properly angled panel and 60–80% real-world efficiency:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Camera type</th><th>Typical daily use</th><th>Typical panel</th><th>Direct sun needed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>2K Wi-Fi battery camera, moderate events</td><td>1–3 Wh</td><td>3–5 W</td><td>About 2–3 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>4K or dual-lens Wi-Fi camera</td><td>2–5 Wh</td><td>5–6 W</td><td>About 3–4 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Built-in solar camera</td><td>1–4 Wh</td><td>Small integrated panel</td><td>About 3–5 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>4G LTE solar camera</td><td>3–8 Wh</td><td>5–10 W</td><td>About 3–5 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Camera with frequent spotlight use</td><td>4–10 Wh</td><td>6–10 W</td><td>About 4+ hours</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>These are illustrative ranges, not specs for any particular model. The sun-hour figures include headroom to refill the battery after a run of cloudy days, which is why they are higher than the bare daily use alone would suggest. Event count is the biggest variable: a camera facing a quiet backyard might wake 10 times a day, while one facing a street might wake 100 times. Peak sun hours also vary from about 2–3 in a northern US or UK winter to 4–6 in summer or in the sunbelt. For location-specific solar data, NREL's <a href="https://pvwatts.nrel.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">PVWatts calculator</a> is a reliable public source. For more on shade and partial sun, read <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-direct-sunlight/">do solar cameras need direct sunlight</a>.</p>
<div class="keyfacts"><div><b>2–4 h</b><span>direct sun a typical 2K camera needs daily</span></div><div><b>60–80%</b><span>of panel rating you get in real life</span></div><div><b>18–37 Wh</b><span>typical camera battery capacity</span></div></div>

<h2>Common mistakes when buying a solar security camera</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Buying before checking sun at the mount</strong><p>A north-facing wall or a spot under a big tree will not charge reliably, no matter how good the camera is. Check the site first, or plan for a detachable panel on a longer cable.</p></li>
<li><strong>Ignoring signal strength</strong><p>A camera with one bar of Wi-Fi retries uploads and drains its battery. Test signal at the exact spot with your phone, and consider a mesh node or a hub.</p></li>
<li><strong>Expecting 24/7 recording</strong><p>Consumer solar cameras are event-based. If you need continuous footage for insurance or a business, look at wired PoE or a larger solar CCTV system. Our <a href="/compare/solar-vs-wired/">solar vs wired comparison</a> covers the trade-offs.</p></li>
<li><strong>Choosing 4K plus a spotlight on a small panel</strong><p>High resolution and color night vision are great, but together on a 3 W panel in a busy area, they often outrun the solar input.</p></li>
<li><strong>Overlooking subscription costs</strong><p>Check which features need a paid plan. A cheap camera with a required subscription can cost more over five years than a pricier one with free local storage.</p></li>
<li><strong>Mounting within reach</strong><p>Low mounts invite theft and tampering. Aim for 7–10 ft (2.1–3 m). Our <a href="/solar-camera-installation/">solar camera installation guide</a> covers mounting height, cable routing and anti-theft steps.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2>Who should skip solar security cameras?</h2>
<p>Solar is not the right answer for every property, and even the best solar CCTV camera cannot fix a site with no sun. Consider a wired system instead if any of these apply:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>You need continuous 24/7 recording</strong> for a business, insurance requirement or high-traffic area.</li>
<li><strong>Your mounting spots are in heavy shade</strong> year-round, and there is no sunny place within cable reach for a panel.</li>
<li><strong>You live far north</strong> with very short winter days and long periods of snow cover, and you cannot accept occasional manual charging.</li>
<li><strong>You need many cameras on one site</strong> with central NVR management, where PoE is cheaper and more reliable per camera.</li>
<li><strong>The camera faces a very busy street</strong> and would wake hundreds of times a day.</li>
</ul>
<p>For everyone else, especially anyone covering spots with no nearby outlet, solar is usually the simplest and cheapest way to add a camera. Our <a href="/tools/solar-vs-wired-cost-calculator/">solar vs wired cost calculator</a> compares five-year costs for your situation.</p>

